
The Linux JDK ports prior to 1.1.3 all suffered from a bug that causes
the getLocalAddress() method of java.net.Socket and java.net.DatagramSocket
to return an InetAddress instance with an IP addresses with a reversed
byte order.  This causes the NetComponents(TM) FTP classes to fail.
The bug is actually in Sun's code, which fails to make a byte-ordering
conversion in one place, causing problems on little-endian architectures.
At least one Linux 1.1.5 port has fixed this problem in the native code.

But if the port you are using has not fixed this problem, to use
NetComponents(TM) on x86 Linux, you must substitute your JDK's 
java.net.PlainSocketImpl and java.net.PlainDatagramSocketImpl .class
files with the ones in the linux/ subdirectory accompanying this
distribution.  The substitute class files provide a temporary workaround 
for the Linux JDK bug.

You MUST install the substitute classes to be sure NetComponents(TM) will
function properly under the Blackdown Linux JDK 1.1.3.  If you are using
Solaris x86, FreeBSD, or any other x86 Unix with a JDK based off of the
Sun JDK sources, you may suffer from the same bug, and might be able to
use our Linux fix.

NOTE TO USERS OF VERSIONS PRIOR TO 1.2.3

We previously provided a workaround using modified InetAddress and
InetAddressImpl classes.  This workaround would make the FTP classes
work, but could break other programs.  If you have the old fix installed,
you should remove it and restore the original InetAddress and
InetAddressImpl classes.  We have traced the bug to the native method
socketGetOption in PlainSocketImpl and have provided an all Java fix
until the Linux JDK's fix the problem in native code.  We apologize for
not getting to the root of the problem sooner.